{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Colorado Rockies were a team that never found solid footing, stumbling out of the gate in April with a 7-20 record that set the tone for most of what followed. The pitching staff was battered from the start, surrendering 957 runs across the season at a bloated 5.67 ERA, and the bullpen — Justin Lawrence in particular — became a recurring symbol of the team's inability to hold games late. April's miseries were embodied by Lawrence surrendering a two-run homer to Nolan Gorman, a sign of things to come from a relief corps that would haunt the Rockies all summer.\n\nMay offered a brief, tantalizing reprieve. The Rockies went 15-13, their only winning month of the season, even as their most impactful plays in that stretch were damaging — Bryce Harper punishing them for a two-run double, and Peter Lambert and Pierce Johnson coughing up home runs. The wins were coming, but they were coming ugly, scraped out despite the bullpen rather than because of it. June brought the season's most electric moment: on June 11th, Coco Montes, Ryan McMahon, and Nolan Jones all went deep in what felt like a rare, joyful eruption from Coors Field. Elias Díaz added a grand slam late in June to keep a flicker of hope alive.\n\nJuly provided one last gasp of drama — C.J. Cron's grand slam on July 16th, followed in the same game by home runs from Nolan Jones and Alan Trejo, felt like the Rockies briefly channeling something special. But the month still ended 9-13, and August was a full-scale collapse at 7-20, with Justin Lawrence suffering hit after hit, Brent Suter surrendering Gunnar Henderson's crusher, and the roster cycling through fringe arms who couldn't stop the bleeding. The Rockies were outscored by 236 runs on the season — a gap that no number of Coors Field fireworks could close.\n\nSeptember mirrored April's futility at 9-19, though a few individual bright spots — Nolan Jones' three-run bomb, Brenton Doyle's clutch triple, and Elehuris Montero's walk-off single on September 15th — gave fans something to cheer in an otherwise lost cause. The Rockies finished 59-103, missing the playoffs by a wide margin, their season a study in pitching fragility and the limits of a thin roster trying to compete in one of baseball's most unforgiving environments.",
